! forms-todo

"Which members of a population still owe a thing for this period, and is the thing they filed real?" — the shape shared by every to-do list of this kind.

!! What the caller binds

* `todo-items` — the items filed for the selected period, as a title list.
* `todo-prior-items` — the items filed for the preceding period.
* `todo-member` — the member of the population a row is about.
* `todo-attribution` — the //name of a function// mapping an item to the member it belongs to. Invoked dynamically, so a caller supplies its own rule for "which service is this review about" without the engine knowing what a service is.
* `todo-template` — optional; a tiddler whose text means "created but not written". Leave blank where there is no template.
* `todo-set-done`, `todo-set-copy`, `todo-set-template` — the rolled-up membership sets, evaluated once per period (below).

!! The states

|!State |!Meaning |
|`missing` |No item for this member and period |
|`template` |An item exists, but its text is still the template's — or empty |
|`copy` |Its text is identical to something from the preceding period |
|`done` |An item exists with content of its own |

`template` is not an edge case wherever guided creation seeds new tiddlers from a template: every item then begins in that state, and a list that counted it as done would go quiet the moment somebody pressed create and wrote nothing.

Comparison is exact, never fuzzy. A near-copy with one word changed reads as `done` — nagging someone who did the work costs more than missing someone who almost didn't.

!! A member with more than one item

One service files one review a month, but it may hold four objectives in a year. So a member's status is the **best** state among its items — one real objective means the year is covered, whatever the other three look like. Precedence runs `done` > `copy` > `template` > `missing`.

`forms-todo-done-set`, `forms-todo-copy-set` and `forms-todo-template-set` are the three disjoint sets, already subtracted by precedence — a page binds them directly as `todo-set-done`, `todo-set-copy` and `todo-set-template` without repeating the subtraction. `forms-todo-status` then answers a row by set membership, and a member lands in exactly one bucket — without the subtraction a service with one written objective and one copied from last year would be counted twice and the summary would exceed the population. The subtraction lives here, once, rather than in every page (and every test) that needs the three sets.

Hoisting matters here. Deriving each row's status from scratch is quadratic — 48 services against 165 objectives, each comparing its text with every objective from the year before — and cost about a second and a half. Evaluating `forms-todo-with-state` once per state, rather than re-deriving per row, brings it to roughly a third of that.

!! Rolling up

`forms-todo-covered` and `forms-todo-with-state` reduce the whole period in one pass. `forms-todo-covered-in` takes any list, which is how a page derives a population — "the members that filed anything at all this year" — from the same attribution rule, so a summary need not re-derive a status per member. Passing the population through the three `-set` functions, and subtracting `forms-todo-covered` for the missing, partitions it exactly.

The state names are identifiers, not labels; a configuration layer maps them to whatever the reader should see.
